#: ../../source/details/spherical_sampling.ipynb:10004
msgid "Uniform Sampling on the Sphere"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/spherical_sampling.ipynb:10006
msgid "This document introduces the method used for uniform sampling on a sphere."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/spherical_sampling.ipynb:10008
msgid "Why do we need uniform sampling on the sphere? The main reasons are:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/spherical_sampling.ipynb:10010
msgid ""
"**Equal-weight sampling over direction**: to sample the original data "
"sufficiently and uniformly in all directions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/spherical_sampling.ipynb:10011
msgid ""
"**Approximate area weighting**: when fitting surfaces, uniform sampling "
"can approximately reproduce weighting by surface area."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/spherical_sampling.ipynb:10013
msgid ""
"At present, there are several possible methods for uniform sampling on "
"the sphere. `Gal3D` adopts a fast and efficient approach: the **Fibonacci"
" sphere sampling** method."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/spherical_sampling.ipynb:10015
msgid ""
"For a set of $N$ sample points, the coordinates $(x_i, y_i, z_i)$ are "
"computed as"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/spherical_sampling.ipynb:10017
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"x_i &= \\sqrt{1 - z_i^2} \\, \\cos\\left(2\\pi i \\phi^{-1}\\right), \\\\"
"\n"
"y_i &= \\sqrt{1 - z_i^2} \\, \\sin\\left(2\\pi i \\phi^{-1}\\right), \\\\"
"\n"
"z_i &= \\frac{2i - 1}{N} - 1,\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/spherical_sampling.ipynb:10025
msgid ""
"where $i = 1, 2, \\ldots, N$, and $\\phi = \\frac{1 + \\sqrt{5}}{2} - 1$ "
"is the inverse of the golden ratio."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/spherical_sampling.ipynb:10029
msgid ""
"[This article](https://extremelearning.com.au/how-to-evenly-distribute-"
"points-on-a-sphere-more-effectively-than-the-canonical-fibonacci-"
"lattice/) provides more background on Fibonacci sphere sampling."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/spherical_sampling.ipynb:10031
msgid ""
"Note that the Fibonacci method implemented in `Gal3D` is equivalent to "
"the Canonical Lattice method described there, namely the Offset Fibonacci"
" Lattice with $\\epsilon = 0.5$."
msgstr ""

